1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Video games can be fun to play.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:08,919
They also can teach people how to settle conflicts peacefully.

3
00:00:08,919 --> 00:00:13,160
This is a video game called Food Force.

4
00:00:13,160 --> 00:00:18,280
It can be downloaded from the website of the World Food Program.

5
00:00:18,280 --> 00:00:25,560
The group's Jennifer Parmaly says the game shows children the difficulty of getting food

6
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:27,560
to areas of conflict.

7
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:31,560
It helps them feel like they could be part of a solution that something was empowering

8
00:00:31,560 --> 00:00:33,560
and fun for them.

9
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:37,560
Another video game is called A Force More Powerful.

10
00:00:37,560 --> 00:00:43,560
It takes place in a city similar to Belgrade in the former Yugoslavia.

11
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:47,560
Even Marovic designed the game ten years ago.

12
00:00:47,560 --> 00:00:54,560
He organized protests against Yugoslav President Slobodan Milosevic.

13
00:00:54,560 --> 00:00:59,560
The president resigned after disputed election results in 2000.

14
00:00:59,560 --> 00:01:06,560
He was arrested on war crimes charges, but died before his trial was completed.

15
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:14,560
Marovic says his game shows non-violent ways to help remove oppressive governments.

16
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:19,560
Like strikes, boycotts, civil disobedience, street protests.

17
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:25,560
In order to force the regime to either concede or to step down.

18
00:01:25,560 --> 00:01:30,560
Marovic says another goal is to win public support.

19
00:01:30,560 --> 00:01:36,560
He says the goal is to win the loyalty of people who support the government.

20
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:41,560
Another goal is to keep up the interest of your current supporters.

21
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:45,560
This game deals with the situation in Darfur, Sudan.

22
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:50,560
The conflict in Darfur has displaced three million people.

23
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:57,560
A website called Second Life was used to create the Darfur video game.

24
00:01:57,560 --> 00:02:05,560
Players can build their own make-believe world and contact others through voice and text messages.

25
00:02:05,560 --> 00:02:11,560
Scott Sexer works for Lyndon Labs, which created Second Life.

26
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:18,560
He says the game lets players come in and see what is taking place in Darfur.

27
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:24,560
Listen to a family which left Darfur and is now in a refugee camp.

28
00:02:24,560 --> 00:02:32,560
The Second Life website is able to change text messages in English to other languages.

29
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:38,560
Sexer says he has communicated with people from around the world.

30
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:45,560
Other parts of Second Life help to support democracy and conflict resolution.

31
00:02:45,560 --> 00:02:53,560
They include a courtroom where people can learn about legal systems in a democracy.

32
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:56,560
I'm Bob Dodie.

